{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NAmTmXSaLk2WcSEosAi6tF","type":"tool_result","content":"b0b5a2a M6: README for the pure JavaScript package\n# Contributing\n\nThanks for helping improve Roast. Bug reports and focused pull requests are\nwelcome. For large features, new dependencies, inference protocols, or changes\nto artifact formats, open an issue before writing the implementation so the\ncontract can be agreed on first.\n\n## Development\n\nContributors need Go 1.27.4 or newer and Node 22.18 or newer; the build\nscripts are TypeScript that Node runs directly. Clone the repository, then:\n\n```sh\nnpm install\nnpm test\nnpm run test:go\nnpm run lint\n```\n\n`npm test` type-checks and bundles the TypeScript CLI, then runs its test\nsuite in Node. `npm run test:go` runs `go test ./...`, compiles the\nlauncher, and runs the launcher and release-script tests with Node's\nbuilt-in test runner. The repository uses npm workspaces over the six\nplatform packages in `packages/`, and `.npmrc` sets `force=true` so npm\nlinks the packages for other platforms too; expect \"using --force\"\nwarnings from every npm command.\n\nDuring the TypeScript rewrite, `npm test` replaces `dist/` with the TS bundle\nat `dist/cli.js`. The package's `roast` command still uses the Go launcher,\nso run a Go build afterward before using `npx roast` in this checkout.\n\nTo build:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build -- --host   # this machine's binary plus the launcher\nnpm run build             # all six targets, license notices, and launcher\n```\n\n`npm run build` cross-compiles `./cmd/roast` with `CGO_ENABLED=0`,\n`-trimpath`, and stripped symbols for darwin, linux, and windows on x64 and\narm64, writing each binary into its platform package's `dist/`. A full build\ntakes several minutes because it generates license notices once per target.\nAfter a build, `npx roast` in this repository runs the local binary through\nthe launcher, the same way it resolves in a user's project. Build before\nusing it: with no built launcher, a `roast` elsewhere on your `PATH` can\nanswer instead.\n\nAlso run these before opening a pull request:\n\n```sh\ngo vet ./...\ngo tool -modfile=.github/tools/staticcheck/go.mod staticcheck ./...\ngo tool -modfile=.github/tools/actionlint/go.mod actionlint\nshellcheck scripts/*.sh\n```\n\nCI-only Go tools have separate modules under `.github/tools/`, maintained by\nRenovate without adding them to Roast's runtime dependencies. Use those modules\nlocally too; a separately installed version can have a different Go importer.\n\nFormat Go changes with `gofmt`. Tests use local HTTP servers and temporary Git\nrepositories; they must not require real model credentials or send repository\ncontent to an inference provider.\n\nOpt-in live tests in `internal/llm` run a minimal two-turn tool loop (one\nexploration call, its result, then the submission on the wrap-up turn) against\na real endpoint with a small output ceiling. They send a fixed probe prompt,\nnever repository content, and skip unless their API key variable is set:\n\n| Variable | Default |\n| --- | --- |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` | unset (skip) |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL` | `https://api.anthropic.com/v1` |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_MODELS` | `claude-sonnet-5-5` (comma-separated) |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` | unset (skip) |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL` | `https://api.deepseek.com/v1` |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_MODEL` | `deepseek-v4-pro` |\n| `ROAST_LIVE_DEEPSEEK_PROTOCOL` | `chat` (or `anthropic` with an `/anthropic/v1` base URL) |\n\n```sh\nROAST_LIVE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=... go test ./internal/llm -run TestLive -count=1 -v\n```\n\nWith a DeepSeek key, a second test makes one structured call with a 256-token\noutput ceiling, which a thinking model spends on reasoning before it calls a\ntool. It checks that the error names the `length` finish and suggests raising\n`max_output_tokens`, and skips if the model answers within the ceiling.\n\nThe Anthropic test also sends the `thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01` beta\nheader without changing the mismatch behavior, so accounts that do not enforce\nthinking-block binding still report a replay whose prefix changed; the test\nfails on any such report. Tests read these variables before clearing the\nambient environment, so other tests never see them. Never commit keys or paste\nthem into logs.\n\nBefore opening a pull request, also check that `go mod tidy` leaves `go.mod`\nand `go.sum` unchanged, including the tool modules (run `go mod tidy` inside\neach tool directory). CI runs vulnerability, Git-history secret, and\ndependency-license checks. New runtime dependencies must use a license\ncompatible with Roast's MIT/Apache-2.0 dual license and should be justified in\nthe pull request.\n\nPull-request CI runs on GitHub-hosted runners with read-only repository\npermissions and no inference or publishing credentials. The `claude-review`\nand `claude-mention` workflows are the exception: they hold an Agent Gateway\ninference credential and can comment on pull requests, but review only\nnon-draft PRs from branches in this repository and answer only commenters with\nwrite access. Keep that boundary: do not run contributor-controlled code in\n`pull_request_target` or a privileged `workflow_run` job. Pin external actions\nto full commit SHAs. Do not bootstrap privileged jobs from pull-request\nartifacts.\n\nKeep changes narrow and include regression tests for behavior changes. Update\nthe README whenever a public flag, configuration field, credential variable,\nartifact, data destination, or exit-code behavior changes. Adding, removing,\nor retyping a project config field also means updating `roast.schema.json`.\n\nPull request titles need a conventional `type:` prefix (such as `feat:`,\n`fix:`, or `docs:`), checked in CI.\n\n## Releases\n\nRoast is published to npm only, from a maintainer's machine; CI never\npublishes. The root package `@substrate-system/roast` holds the launcher and\nthe config schema, and the six `@substrate-system/roast-<os>-<arch>` packages\neach hold one binary and its license notices.\n\n```sh\nnpm version <new-version>\nnpm run release\n```\n\n`npm version` runs the `version` lifecycle script, which writes the new\nversion into every platform package and into the root package's\n`optionalDependencies`, so the version commit includes them all.\n`npm run release` runs `npm run build`, publishes the platform packages\nthrough the workspaces, then publishes the root package. Extra arguments\n(such as `--dry-run`) go to both publish steps. The root package's\n`prepublishOnly` refuses to publish unless all six binaries and their license\nnotices exist and every version matches.\n\n## Dependency notices\n\n`bash scripts/licenses.sh [<goos>/<goarch> ...]` checks and collects runtime\ndependency licenses and notices for the CGO-disabled release targets (all six\nby default). It uses the `go-licenses/v2` tool module in\n`.github/tools/licenses`, generates per-target CSV inventories, and includes\nthe Go runtime/standard-library license. Generated files live in the ignored\n`THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/` directory, separate from Roast's own licenses. The\nscript refuses to replace a directory it did not generate, and failed\ngeneration leaves any previous bundle intact.\n\n`npm run build` runs the script once per target and copies that target's\nnotices into its platform package's `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES/`. When a\ndependency drops out of the `./cmd/roast` graph, check\n`go list -deps ./cmd/roast` for each target and update any notice the script\nspecial-cases. When changing dependencies, inspect the generated inventories;\nautomated classification is not a substitute for legal review.\n\nPrefer tagged stable dependency releases. Explain any necessary development pin\nor prerelease in its pull request and revisit it when a stable release permits.\nRerun provider tests, module checks, vulnerability checks, and notice generation\nafter dependency changes; old advisory notes are not a substitute for scanning\nthe selected module graph.\n\n## Security\n\nDo not report vulnerabilities in a public issue. Follow [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md)\ninstead.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc9cd1ee-6217-41cc-8c13-e5fed07b92d5","uuid":"d5d198d3-f124-4848-9179-05f99797e542","timestamp":"2026-11-24T03:55:30.391Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"b0b5a2a M6: README for the pure JavaScript package\n# Contributing\n\nThanks for helping improve Roast. Bug reports and focused pull requests are\nwelcome. For large features, new dependencies, inference protocols, or changes\nto artifact formats, open an issue before writing the implementation so the\ncontract can be agreed on first.\n\n## Development\n\nContributors need Go 1.27.4 or newer and Node 22.18 or newer; the build\nscripts are TypeScript that Node runs directly. Clone the repository, then:\n\n```sh\nnpm install\nnpm test\nnpm run test:go\nnpm run lint\n```\n\n`npm test` type-checks and bundles the TypeScript CLI, then runs its test\nsuite in Node. `npm run test:go` runs `go test ./...`, compiles the\nlauncher, and runs the launcher and release-script tests with Node's\nbuilt-in test runner. The repository uses npm workspaces over the six\nplatform packages in `packages/`, and `.npmrc` sets `force=true` so npm\nlinks the packages for other platforms too; expect \"using --force\"\nwarnings from every npm command.\n\nDuring the TypeScript rewrite, `npm test` replaces `dist/` with the TS bundle\nat `dist/cli.js`. The package's `roast` command still uses the Go launcher,\nso run a Go build afterward before using `npx roast` in this checkout.\n\nTo build:\n\n```sh\nnpm run build -- --host   # this machine's binary plus the launcher\nnpm run build             # all six targets, license notices, and launcher\n```\n\n`npm run build` cross-compiles `./cmd/roast` with `CGO_ENABLED=0`,\n`-trimpath`, and stripped symbols fo